**Diagnosis:** the 3 managed identities were created. Key Vault was denied by policy `az-024` (Deny): `networkAcls.defaultAction` must be `Deny`. My command left it at the default `Allow`. It is fixable without an SR.

**Quick patch (retry with the firewall on):**
```bash
az keyvault create -g PZI-GX-P-RGP-MDCL-P002 -n kv-cn-stg-uae -l uaenorth \
  --enable-rbac-authorization true \
  --enable-purge-protection true \
  --retention-days 90 \
  --default-action Deny \
  --bypass AzureServices
```
(GUI equivalent: Key Vault → Create → Networking tab → "Enable public access from specific virtual networks and IP addresses", with no networks added.)

| Part | Impact |
|---|---|
| `--default-action Deny` | All network access is blocked unless allowed by a rule. This satisfies az-024 |
| `--bypass AzureServices` | Trusted Microsoft services can still reach the vault |

⚠️ With Deny, **your own Cloud Shell can't read or write secrets** (data plane). Creating the vault and role assignments still works, because that is the management plane. Don't store secrets yet. To set them later, add your current IP: `az keyvault network-rule add -n kv-cn-stg-uae --ip-address <IP>/32`. The Cloud Shell IP changes per session, so this is temporary.

⚠️ Purge protection is irreversible, so confirm the name `kv-cn-stg-uae` before running.

**Long-term (recommended for prod):** a private endpoint, or a VNet rule with a `Microsoft.KeyVault` service endpoint on the app subnets. Both need the VNet, so add them to the networking SR.

